const TA_COMMIT = "b42d324";
const TA_KB = [{"id":"lead-times","title":"Expected Delivery Lead Times — Portable, Custom, and Build-On-Site","topic":"Delivery and site","aud":"staff","src":"ShedCor","slug":"expected-inventory-lead-times-order-to-lot-delivery-v1","hint":"In-stock portable sheds: 1–2 weeks. Custom orders: 2–4 weeks. Build-on-site: 2–4 weeks to production then scheduled directly with customer. Delivery free within 50 miles; $100 for 50–60 miles; beyond 60 miles requires manager approval.","body":"## In-Stock Portable Buildings\n\nIn-stock portable buildings are typically delivered within **1–2 weeks** of purchase through Bennett Buildings, subject to driver and route availability.\n\n## Custom-Ordered Portable Buildings\n\nCustom-ordered portable buildings carry a lead time of **2–4 weeks** from order placement to delivery. This timeline is set by ShedCor's production schedule and is inherited by all Bennett Buildings locations.\n\n## Common Reasons for Delay\n\nCertain options extend lead time beyond the standard window:\n- **Vinyl siding** — carries an advertised production delay; customers should be informed at time of order.\n- **Heavily custom or non-standard options** not covered in standard pricing — may require additional production coordination; timeline will be communicated at time of order.\n- **Rain or severe weather** — can delay delivery and/or build crew scheduling.\n\n## Build-On-Site Orders\n\nBuild-on-site orders follow the same **2–4 week production window** as custom portable orders. After production is complete, the installation phase is handled separately:\n- A scheduler contacts the customer directly to agree on an installation date.\n- Customers should expect approximately **2 weeks from production completion to initial scheduling contact**, with the goal of reaching a mutually agreed date within 2 additional weeks.\n- Actual installation dates are subject to weather conditions and crew travel availability.\n\n## Delivery Distance and Fees\n\n- **Within 50 miles** of the sales office: delivery is included at no additional charge.\n- **50–60 miles**: a flat **$100 delivery fee** applies.\n- **Beyond 60 miles**: non-standard; requires manager approval. Management will determine and quote the upcharge on a case-by-case basis.\n\n## Questions About a Specific Delivery\n\nFor questions about a specific delivery issue or delay, use the **Contacts directory** and search the tag **delivery** to find the appropriate point of contact for your location.\n\n## Slang / Alternate Phrasing Support\n\nQuestions like \"how do I get an update on a custom build\" should be interpreted as a **status request on a custom-order timeline**.\n\nResponse pattern:\n- Confirm custom-order lead-time baseline (**2-4 weeks**, unless delayed by options/weather).\n- Explain that build-on-site scheduling happens after production.\n- Route exact order-status checks through local operations flow (Contacts tag **delivery** or **admin**, depending on office workflow).","tags":["operations","lead_time","delivery","inventory","portable","build-on-site","fees"],"flag":"This preview charges what the owner confirmed: free to 50 miles, $100 for 51 to 100 miles, and past 100 miles Logistics approves a fee. This article says $100 for 50 to 60 miles and manager approval past 60."},{"id":"size-limits","title":"Portable Shed Size Range and Delivery Limits","topic":"Sizes and styles","aud":"both","src":"ShedCor","slug":"kb-products-shed-size-range-portable","hint":"Routine maximum delivered portable size is 14×40. Sixteen-foot-wide buildings are build-on-site only. Fourteen-foot-wide is deliverable but not standard inventory (custom order or sell if already on lot). Site access can still block delivery.","body":"## Largest Size We Routinely Deliver\n\nThe standard maximum **delivered** portable building size is **14 feet wide by 40 feet long**. Length is usually limited by common trailer capacity (~40 ft loads). Width and overall height are constrained by **DOT transport and permitting**, not customer preference alone.\n\nRare exceptions (for example, a longer trailer in the network) are **not** promised in customer conversations. If a customer insists on an edge case, **escalate to dealer support** so ownership can evaluate permitting, cost, and timeline.\n\n## Sixteen-Foot-Wide Buildings\n\n**All 16-wide buildings are build-on-site only** under standard policy. Do **not** describe delivered 16-wide sheds as a normal option.\n\nIf a customer demands a non-standard path and is willing to pay permitting and wait for special handling, that still routes through **dealer support / ownership**—it is not a self-serve exception.\n\n## Fourteen-Foot-Wide Buildings\n\n**14-wide is deliverable** and does **not** require build-on-site.\n\n**Inventory:** 14-wide is **generally not stocked** because permitting costs make it inefficient to pay for a permit to the sales lot and again for customer delivery. **Custom order** is normal.\n\nIf a **14-wide is already on a lot** (repo, rare leadership exception, or flipped inventory unit), **sell it like other inventory** unless leadership has marked it otherwise.\n\n## Inventory Widths (Typical)\n\nWidths **up to 12 feet** are standard inventory candidates: **6-wide** exists as a small subset; **8, 10, and 12** are the main inventory-eligible widths.\n\n**14-wide** is treated as **custom order** for new stock, not standard inventory purchasing, for the permitting-cost reasons above.\n\n## Site Access Can Override Size Rules\n\nEven when a building is within legal transport size, **delivery may fail** if the site or route is unsafe or impossible. Common constraints: **narrow streets or HOA gates**, **low trees or power lines**, **tight turns**, **soft or wet yards**, **septic or sprinkler lines**, and similar obstructions.\n\n**Hard or concrete ground** is strongly preferred; **gravel** is often acceptable; **soft ground** after rain is high risk.\n\nIf the customer overrides driver advice and equipment gets stuck or the yard is damaged, **recovery and yard repair are the customer's responsibility**. If delivery was attempted **against prior advice** and cannot be completed, **redelivery fees** may apply; amounts are set by delivery companies—coordinate through **delivery / admin** (Contacts tag **delivery**).\n\n## Modular or Split Delivery\n\n**Modular (split + set) oversized buildings are not offered today.** Anything beyond routine transport limits must be **build-on-site**.\n\nIf product strategy changes later, update this article—until then the answer is **no**.\n\n## Not Sold as a Dwelling\n\nPortable buildings are **not living spaces as sold**. Converting one for occupancy requires **local permits**, **licensed work**, **jurisdiction approval**, and (for rent-to-own) **ownership** before structural or permanent interior changes. See RTO and tiny-home policy articles for financing and modification rules.","tags":["portable","delivery","inventory","size","14-wide","16-wide","DOT"]},{"id":"roof-rules","title":"Roof Styles and Height / Door Rules (Portable Line)","topic":"Sizes and styles","aud":"both","src":"ShedCor","slug":"kb-products-roof-styles","hint":"Standard sidewall height is 6' on most lines; lofted barn interior peak ~10', exterior ~11'4\" (does not vary by width). Short-wall barn uses 4' sidewalls. Tall wall is excluded from greenhouses, Craftsman Shake, and mini barn. Tall wall studio: roll-up garage door on the high sidewall. Tall wall utility: sidewall roll-up allowed. No roll-up on barns. No dormers on barn style.","body":"## Standard Sidewall and Lofted Barn Heights\n\nOn standard series without the tall-wall upgrade, **sidewall height is 6 feet** on most portable lines.\n\n**Lofted barn:** interior peak is about **10 feet**; from the ground to exterior peak is about **11 feet 4 inches**. **Peak height does not change with building width**—only truss layout adjusts.\n\nWhere the detail matters for sales, interior clearance **above the double top plate** on a lofted barn is about **6 feet 4 inches** below the loft framing (customers often ask about standing room).\n\n## Short-Wall Barn\n\nThe **short-wall barn** uses approximately **4-foot** sidewalls. It is intentionally a shorter profile for customers who want a barn look with reduced wall height.\n\n## Tall Wall Upgrade — Where It Applies\n\n**Tall wall** is a standard upgrade across **Basic, Classic, and Elite** (all roof styles) and **Silver Bullet** metal buildings.\n\n**Tall wall is not available on:** **greenhouses**, **Craftsman Shake series**, or **mini barn** models.\n\n**Doghouse** add-ons follow the **Basic/Classic/Elite** family they attach to—tall wall follows that parent series.\n\nTall wall raises the structure and **triggers higher permitting / transport tiers** where applicable. **14-wide** and **tall wall barn** permitting tiers are designed so customers are **not double-charged** the same permit add-on when both apply—quote systems should reflect the combined rule.\n\n## Which Buildings Feel Largest Inside\n\n**Tall wall barn** has the greatest peak and overall interior height.\n\n**Tall wall utility or studio** often **feels** the most usable day to day for storage and workshop layouts.\n\n**Classic and Elite** interiors can **feel larger** than Basic because of **TechShield** wall and ceiling treatment (radiant barrier backing)—see materials article.\n\n## Roll-Up and Garage Doors\n\n**Barn-style buildings:** **no** roll-up or garage door on the barn sidewalls.\n\n**Tall wall utility:** roll-up / garage door on the **sidewall** is allowed.\n\n**Tall wall studio:** roll-up / garage door is allowed on the **high sidewall** (the taller studio wall).\n\nOther door types follow normal option guides unless a package explicitly restricts them.\n\n## Dormers\n\n**Dormers are not supported on barn-style buildings.**\n\n## Lofts and Customer Framing\n\nLofted barns include lofts by design. Customers who **own** the building may add interior framing or finishes consistent with warranty and finance rules.\n\n**Rent-to-own:** **no modifications** that permanently alter the building until the customer **owns** it outright. See RTO operational articles.","tags":["roof","loft","barn","tall_wall","doors","dimensions"]},{"id":"set-height","title":"Installation — Set Height, Blocking, and Site Conditions","topic":"Delivery and site","aud":"both","src":"ShedCor","slug":"longhorn-payment-installation-and-warranty-terms-v1","hint":"Measure set height from the highest ground point under the building to the bottom of the runners: 2\" standard block at that point, maximum 16\" at that point. Do not exceed 16\" of blocking at the high point. Downhill sides may be taller below the runners when leveling on slope. Crew does not grade the site.","body":"## How Set Height Is Measured\n\nSet height is measured from **ground level at the highest point under the building** to the **bottom of the runners**.\n\n**Standard install:** about **2 inches** of leveling block at that **highest** ground contact point, then the structure is leveled with block and shims toward lower sides.\n\n**Maximum at the high point:** **16 inches** from ground to the bottom of the runners. **Do not stack blocks higher than 16 inches at that highest ground contact point.** This is a **hard safety rule** for structure stability and installer safety working beneath the skid.\n\n## Sloped Lots\n\nOn slopes, the **downhill** corners can show **more than 16 inches** of space below the runners—that is normal when the building is leveled to the **high corner** with the 2-inch / 16-inch rule applied **at the high corner only**.\n\nSlope and terrain **do not** change the **16-inch limit at the highest ground point**; they change how much blocking appears on the low side.\n\n## Customer Requests Above Standard Blocking\n\nAnything beyond **standard blocking and leveling** is **quoted separately** and must be **paid before delivery** when pre-arranged. Customers can call before delivery to add paid block if the order is updated in time.\n\nIf a customer asks to **stack blocks above the 16-inch high-point limit**, the answer is **no**—policy and safety do not allow it.\n\n## Site Prep Responsibilities\n\nDelivery crews **level the building with block**; they **do not regrade** the customer's yard.\n\nSites should be **accessible** and **as level as practical**, but sheds can often be blocked to moderate slopes.\n\n**Concrete pads** are **recommended** (smooth, stable, easy delivery) but **not required**. **Gravel** pads are acceptable when stable.\n\n**Manufacturer/dealer does not guarantee third-party site-prep contractors.** Some offices maintain local referrals, but outcomes are between the customer and the contractor.","tags":["installation","set_height","blocking","site_prep","foundation"]},{"id":"materials","title":"LP SmartSide, TechShield, and Climate / Insulation Options","topic":"Materials and warranty","aud":"both","src":"ShedCor","slug":"kb-products-materials-lp-smartside","hint":"Classic and Elite include TechShield radiant barrier on walls and ceiling; Basic and Silver Bullet do not. No full insulation package is standard. Optional bubble insulation and spray foam services exist. There is no single published R-value for the building as sold—use LP/TechShield docs for component data.","body":"## What Ships Standard\n\nWood portable buildings are **not sold as fully insulated residential envelopes**. They are engineered utility structures with siding, venting, and (on some tiers) **radiant barrier** sheathing.\n\n## TechShield (Classic and Elite)\n\n**TechShield** is the branded **radiant-barrier** roof/wall sheathing used on **Classic and Elite** series. It is installed on **walls and ceiling** in those tiers.\n\n**Basic** wood series and **Silver Bullet** metal buildings **do not** include TechShield as described here.\n\nTechShield **reduces radiant heat gain** and can modestly improve comfort, but it is **not a substitute** for full cavity insulation unless the customer finishes and insulates the building properly.\n\n## Optional Insulation Upgrades\n\nDealers can sell **bubble insulation** packages and **spray foam insulation services** on many builds. These are **true insulation upgrades** compared with radiant barrier alone.\n\nAlways quote and document upgrades in the sales platform so production and install match the order.\n\n## Answering “What Is the R-Value?”\n\nThere is **no single R-value assigned to the shed as sold** because packages vary and walls are not filled with standard batt insulation by default.\n\nFor customers who need numbers, point them to **LP SmartSide / TechShield manufacturer documentation** for **component** R-values or performance data.\n\nAvoid guaranteeing specific indoor/outdoor temperature deltas—conditions, doors left open, and ventilation change results.\n\n## Customer-Added Insulation and Warranty\n\nAdding insulation **by itself** does not automatically void the warranty.\n\n**Poorly executed insulation**—especially **closed-cell foam** without moisture planning—can trap moisture, damage finishes, or create conditions that **void warranty coverage for resulting damage**.\n\nRecommend **licensed installers** and ventilation planning when customers finish interiors.\n\n## Wood vs Silver Bullet (Heat Perception)\n\n**Wood buildings generally stay cooler in summer** than **Silver Bullet** metal buildings in typical use. This is a **general comparison**, not a guaranteed performance claim—always consider ventilation, color, and whether doors are open.","tags":["materials","LP","TechShield","insulation","radiant_barrier","climate"]},{"id":"wind-anchors","title":"Portable Buildings — Wind Ratings, Engineering Documents, and Anchors","topic":"Materials and warranty","aud":"both","src":"ShedCor","slug":"shedcor-portable-wind-engineering-and-anchors-v1","hint":"Wind engineering coverage applies to Basic, Classic, and Elite across Utility, Barn, and Studio roof families, plus Silver Bullet series and upgrade options. Certifications commonly reference nominal tiers up to 133 MPH and 150 MPH, with anchoring schedules driving the main difference. Anchors are priced separately.","body":"## Nominal Wind Certifications and Product Scope\n\nWind engineering coverage applies to **Basic, Classic, and Elite** portable families across **Utility, Barn, and Studio** roof styles, plus **Silver Bullet** series and their documented upgrade option sets.\n\nThese families have engineering documentation supporting nominal certifications commonly referenced up to **133 MPH** and up to **150 MPH**, depending on package and anchoring schedule.\n\nThe structural build intent is largely shared across these tiers; **anchoring schedules** are the primary driver of the rating difference.\n\n## Anchors Are Not Automatic\n\n**Anchors are not included standard.** They are **priced separately** and often treated as **customer/discretionary** unless a jurisdiction mandates tie-downs.\n\nAnchoring can be done **at initial set** or on a **return trip** (trip fee may apply). Anchor pricing is quoted on request—do **not** invent dollar amounts in KB answers.\n\n## Engineering Document Tiers\n\nCustomers may need:\n\n1. **Generic** engineering package — baseline documents for the series.\n2. **Site-specific** package — generic calculations with **customer site and name** filled in for permit offices that require it.\n3. **Building-specific** package — full custom document set for demanding jurisdictions.\n\nRequirements vary **county by county**—there is no universal map of who needs which tier. **Dealers should request guidance from the permit office** and escalate to **engineering / manufacturer support** when unsure.\n\n## Customer Questions About Hurricanes\n\nPortable buildings are engineered to **documented wind speeds** when installed per engineering, including proper anchoring.\n\n**Do not guarantee** survival in **every** hurricane, **flood**, or **tree impact**. Real storms include debris, water loads, and micro-bursts beyond nominal ratings.\n\nSafe script pattern: compare expectations to **local residential code**, emphasize **anchors**, and remind customers that **engineering PDFs** are the authority if an inspector asks.\n\n## Steel Structures Use Different Engineering\n\n**Steel carports and rigid-frame buildings** use **separate engineering packages** and vendors. Do not copy wood portable wind statements onto steel SKUs—route questions to steel engineering articles.","tags":["wind","engineering","anchors","permits","structural"]},{"id":"down-payment","title":"Down Payment Requirements — Cash and Rent-to-Own Overview","topic":"Payment and rent-to-own","aud":"both","src":"ShedCor","slug":"shedcor-down-payment-requirements-v1","hint":"Cash sales: 10% deposit can hold inventory or start a build; remaining balance is due before delivery for typical residential customers. RTO: first payment(s) act as deposit at order; first recurring payment after deposit is about 30 days after delivery. Nothing schedules until admin has complete paperwork.","body":"## Cash Sales — Deposits and Final Payment\n\n**Cash purchases** typically require a **10% deposit** to **hold inventory** or **authorize production** on a custom order.\n\nFor **typical residential customers**, the **remaining balance must be paid before delivery is scheduled**, even if the customer already inspected the building on the lot.\n\nCustomers may pay the balance after viewing a unit on the lot, or pay before **plant-direct** delivery—either path still ends with **paid-in-full before the truck rolls to the home**.\n\n## Rare Exceptions (Commercial / Government)\n\nA **very small number** of **government or invoice-based commercial** deals may arrange **final payment on delivery** because of billing constraints.\n\n**Residential managers must not promise** this pathway. Escalate unusual billing requirements to **admin / leadership**.\n\n## Rent-to-Own — Deposit Concept\n\nOn **RTO**, the **first payment** (sometimes the **first two payments**, or higher amounts on certain tiers) collected **at order** **replaces** the cash deposit/down-payment pattern for scheduling purposes.\n\nDetails vary by contract tier—see **Rent-to-Own operational** and **payment timing** articles.\n\n## Nothing Moves Until Paperwork Is Complete\n\n**Do not schedule delivery** until **admin** confirms **all required payments for the stage** and **all deal documentation** are complete for **cash, finance, or RTO**.\n\nIf training references “payment before delivery,” treat it as **policy**, not a suggestion.","tags":["payment","deposit","cash","rto","delivery"]},{"id":"rto-overview","title":"Rent-To-Own Financing — Customer Overview","topic":"Payment and rent-to-own","aud":"both","src":"ShedCor","slug":"kb-financing-rent-to-own","hint":"RTO is offered through JMAG. Deposit is due at order, then regular monthly payments (same minimum as base contract) continue during the 90-day SAC window. If SAC voids, amounts beyond deposit plus the three required SAC payments move to CSRA. CSRA can cover delinquent payments only with customer permission; for account-specific details, customers should contact JMAG.","body":"## Provider\n\nPortable-building **rent-to-own (RTO)** for the ShedCor dealer program is fulfilled through **JMAG**.\n\n## Payment Timing (What Customers Feel)\n\n- **At order:** pay the **RTO deposit**, which is usually the **first payment** and sometimes the **first two payments** depending on plan tier.\n- **After delivery:** the **first regular recurring payment** is due about **30 days after delivery** (confirm exact due date on contract).\n\nUntil **deposit payments and paperwork** are complete, **delivery is not scheduled**.\n\n## Ninety-Day Same-As-Cash (SAC)\n\nJMAG contracts include **90-day same-as-cash** language so customers may **pay off the cash balance** within the first **90 days** while staying current.\n\nDuring SAC, customers still owe the **same minimum monthly payment as the base contract** each month.\n\nIf the customer **defaults or hits collections inside that 90-day window**, **same-as-cash benefits are void** and the deal continues under **standard RTO terms**.\n\nWhen SAC voids, payments beyond the **deposit** and the **three required SAC-period payments** are treated as **excess** and move into the **CSRA** balance under lessor rules.\n\nCustomer-facing expectation: only CSRA amounts captured at deposit time reduce contract principal; excess money from individual payments or SAC-timeout default does not automatically reduce principal the same way.\n\n## Early Payoff and Switching to Cash\n\nCustomers may **pay off early** by contacting **JMAG** for a payoff quote. Payoff amounts **differ from cash retail** unless the customer pays within the **SAC window**.\n\nDeals can be **converted from RTO to cash** administratively when the customer brings funds—coordinate through **admin / JMAG**.\n\n## Missed Payments and CSRA Use\n\nThe RTO company **prefers to keep customers in the building** and usually reaches out when a payment is missed.\n\nIf enough **CSRA** balance exists, JMAG may ask the customer whether they want to apply those funds to **cover or fully cure delinquent payments**. That application requires **customer permission**.\n\nIf a customer falls **about two payments behind**, repossession processes can begin. Do not promise payment holidays—customers facing hardship should contact **JMAG** directly.\n\nOptional **liability damage waiver (LDW)** products from the RTO provider may cover some **acts of God**; they are **not** the manufacturer warranty.\n\n## Sales Role vs RTO Company Role\n\nSales staff should be knowledgeable enough to explain the major terms of the contract before signing, including deposit timing, SAC basics, and modification restrictions.\n\nSales staff are **not** RTO company account specialists. After purchase, detailed account-specific questions (past-due handling, CSRA ledger application, payoff math) should be routed to **JMAG**.\n\n## Is There an Interest Rate?\n\n**No.** JMAG/ShedCor rent-to-own is **not an interest-based credit product**. It is a **month-to-month lease agreement** that reinstates with each successful monthly payment until payoff, voluntary return, or involuntary repo under contract terms.\n\nUse this language to avoid framing RTO like a bank loan APR product.","tags":["rto","financing","JMAG","same_as_cash","payments"]},{"id":"rto-rules","title":"Rent-To-Own — Operational Rules (Dealer Staff)","topic":"Payment and rent-to-own","aud":"staff","src":"ShedCor","slug":"rent-to-own-operational-rules-shedcor-program","hint":"JMAG is the RTO provider. Collect deposit payments per plan (cash, card, certified check; personal checks only after clearing). During SAC, base monthly minimums still apply. If SAC voids, excess above deposit plus required SAC payments moves to CSRA. CSRA can cure delinquency only with customer authorization.","body":"## Provider and Scope\n\nThis article covers **dealer staff operations** for portable-building **RTO** through **JMAG**. If the organization adds another lessor later, split articles per provider.\n\n## Accepted Deposit Payment Types\n\nJMAG deposits may be paid via **cash**, **credit/debit card**, and **certified check**.\n\n**Personal checks** may be accepted only when policy allows, and the deposit is **not considered paid until the check clears**.\n\n## Scheduling Gate\n\n**Inventory and custom orders** must not move to delivery scheduling until:\n\n1. Required **RTO deposit payments** are collected per the plan, and\n2. **Admin** confirms **all contract paperwork** is complete and funded correctly.\n\nThis mirrors the cash-sale rule—**no paperwork, no truck**.\n\n## Same-As-Cash Compliance\n\nEvery new JMAG contract should be treated as carrying **90-day SAC** rights if the customer stays current.\n\nDuring SAC, the customer still owes the **base-contract minimum monthly payment** each month.\n\nIf SAC is voided (default / collections), document the account as standard RTO. Per current guidance, amounts above the **deposit** plus the **three required SAC-period payments** are treated as **excess** and moved to **CSRA** under lessor accounting rules.\n\nDo not improvise alternate CSRA math—if contract-level details are disputed, escalate to finance admin or JMAG.\n\nOperational note: only deposit-captured CSRA is treated as principal-reducing. Excess collected through individual payments or SAC-timeout default follows lessor ledger rules and should not be quoted as principal reduction unless JMAG confirms.\n\n## Delinquency Workflow and CSRA Authorization\n\nWhen customers miss payments, JMAG typically contacts them first. If sufficient **CSRA** balance exists, JMAG may offer to apply CSRA to **partially or fully cure delinquency**, but only with **customer permission**.\n\nEscalate stalled accounts early. Internal shorthand remains that around two missed payments increases formal repo risk, but always verify timing with JMAG before quoting dates.\n\nRemind customers that **LDW** (if purchased) is not the same as manufacturer defect warranty.\n\n## Modifications and Recovery\n\nDocument that **RTO customers lack ownership** until payoff. If a repo occurs on a modified unit, **damage or value loss** may be billed per contract.\n\nWhen in doubt, **photo-document** the building at delivery and at any service visit.","tags":["rto","JMAG","operations","admin","compliance"]},{"id":"rto-timing","title":"Rent-To-Own Payment Timing and Schedule (Reference)","topic":"Payment and rent-to-own","aud":"staff","src":"ShedCor","slug":"shedcor-rto-payment-timing-and-schedule-v1","hint":"Deposit payments at order (often first or first two payments). First recurring payment after that is approximately 30 days post-delivery. Verify dates on the signed contract and in the POS.","body":"## At Order\n\nCollect the **RTO deposit** defined by the plan—commonly the **first payment** or **first two payments**. Some price tiers require **higher initial payments**.\n\n**No delivery scheduling** until **admin** validates paperwork and funding.\n\n## After Delivery\n\nThe **first payment after the deposit cycle** is typically due about **30 days after delivery**.\n\nAlways confirm the **exact due date** on the **customer contract** and in **inventory host / finance screens** rather than estimating in chat.\n\n## Customer FAQ Tie-Ins\n\n- **“Can I get delivery before I pay anything?”** — **No.** Deposit and paperwork must clear first.\n- **“Is my first bill due on delivery day?”** — **Usually no**—expect about **30 days after delivery** for the first recurring payment after deposit, unless the contract states otherwise.\n- **“Can I pay off early?”** — **Yes**, via JMAG payoff quote.","tags":["rto","schedule","payments","JMAG"]},{"id":"greenhouse","title":"Greenhouse Building System (Pricing Clarification)","topic":"Sizes and styles","aud":"both","src":"ShedCor","slug":"greenhouse-building-system","hint":"The greenhouse exhaust fan is included in the base greenhouse price, not priced as a separate add-on line item.","body":"## Exhaust Fan Pricing Clarification\n\nFor the standard greenhouse configuration, the **exhaust fan is included in the base building price**. It is not quoted as a separate standalone add-on in normal greenhouse pricing.\n\nIf future product updates create optional fan upgrades or alternate fan tiers, pricing should be published in the pricing lane rather than guessed in KB answers.","tags":["greenhouse","options","pricing","ventilation"]},{"id":"lost-keys","title":"Lost Keys and Lockset Replacement Policy (Portable Buildings)","topic":"Materials and warranty","aud":"both","src":"ShedCor","slug":"shedcor-lost-keys-and-lockset-replacement-v1","hint":"ShedCor cannot supply master keys or new key sets for existing handles. If keys are lost or missing, sell and install a new matching T-handle or lockset through a repair request managed by sales.","body":"## Key Replacement Rules\n\nShedCor does **not** supply master keys and does **not** reissue key sets for an existing installed handle.\n\n## What To Offer Instead\n\nIf keys are lost, stolen, or missing, the standard fix is to sell a **new lockset** (for example, a new T-handle set or compatible door-knob set) with matching keys.\n\n## Workflow\n\nA **sales manager** handles this by submitting a **repair request** with the building details and lockset requirement. This applies to customer-owned buildings and inventory units with misplaced/missing keys.","tags":["keys","locks","repairs","service"]},{"id":"shedcor","title":"Manufacturing Arm: ShedCor","topic":"Company and CRM","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-company-manufacturing-shedcor","hint":"ShedCor is a manufacturer serving a dealer network with customizable portable buildings; Bennett is one dealer-side retail operation, while ShedCor remains dealer-first and does not sell direct to end users.","body":"ShedCor is a portable-building manufacturer focused on high-quality, highly customizable products distributed through a dealer network.\n\nShedCor does not sell directly to end users. ShedCor's direct business customer is the dealer, and its manufacturing mission is to help dealers serve end customers with strong product options, trusted warranty coverage, dependable service, and competitive pricing.\n\nBennett is a retail company, while ShedCor is the manufacturer. Although the organizations share ownership context and some people, ShedCor maintains dealer/manufacturer boundaries so dealers can trust long-term channel fairness.\n\nShedCor positions itself as a growth partner for ambitious dealers: under-promise/over-deliver execution, dependable support, and dealer-brand enablement so both manufacturer and dealer can grow together.\n\nBennett is used as an early pilot environment for programs that can later benefit the broader dealer network, with the intent that dealers receive the same level of support and opportunity.","tags":["company","manufacturing","shedcor","production"]},{"id":"bennett","title":"Company Overview: Bennett Buildings","topic":"Company and CRM","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-company-overview-bennett-buildings","hint":"Bennett Buildings is a retail company specializing in the sale of portable sheds and buildings delivered fully constructed to customer sites.","body":"Bennett Buildings is a retail company specializing in the sale of portable sheds and buildings delivered fully constructed to customer sites.","tags":["company","overview","bennett","retail","portable-buildings"]},{"id":"crm-followup","title":"CRM Automated Follow-Up","topic":"Company and CRM","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-crm-automation-follow-up","hint":"The CRM supports automated texts, emails, and reminders to ensure consistent lead follow-up.","body":"The CRM supports automated texts, emails, and reminders to ensure consistent lead follow-up.","tags":["crm","automation","follow-up"]},{"id":"crm-lead","title":"CRM Lead Definition","topic":"Company and CRM","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-crm-leads-definition","hint":"A lead is any actionable customer inquiry or contact intent that should enter the CRM with owner, stage, and next-step accountability.","body":"A lead is any customer contact or inquiry with potential buying intent that should be tracked through the sales process. Leads can originate from website forms, ads, phone/text conversations, walk-ins, referrals, or manual entry.\n\nMinimum lead record quality includes identifiable contact information, inquiry context (what they want), assigned owner, and current stage so follow-up is operationally actionable.\n\nA lead is not considered complete without a next action and due date. Untasked leads create follow-up risk and should be corrected immediately.\n\nQualification status can evolve over time, but early capture is preferred. It is better to create and triage a lead than to delay entry and risk losing customer context.\n\nCustomer-facing impact: accurate lead definition and entry improves response speed, reduces dropped opportunities, and keeps handoffs consistent across team members.","tags":["crm","leads","definitions"]},{"id":"crm-pipelines","title":"CRM Pipelines","topic":"Company and CRM","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-crm-pipelines","hint":"Pipelines track lead progression through stages of the sales process from inquiry to close.","body":"Pipelines track lead progression through stages of the sales process from inquiry to close.","tags":["crm","pipelines","sales-process"]},{"id":"crm-ghl","title":"CRM Platform: GoHighLevel","topic":"Company and CRM","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-crm-platform-go-high-level","hint":"GoHighLevel is the CRM platform used for lead intake, pipeline tracking, communication history, and follow-up task execution across the sales lifecycle.","body":"Bennett uses GoHighLevel (GHL) as its CRM platform for managing leads, contacts, conversations, follow-up tasks, and pipeline progression from first inquiry through close/loss outcomes.\n\nCore practical use areas include lead capture, ownership assignment, stage tracking, note logging, communication timeline visibility, and scheduled next actions so team members can execute timely follow-up.\n\nPrimary users typically include sales reps, managers, and marketing/ops support roles who depend on shared CRM visibility for handoffs, accountability, and reporting.\n\nHard usage rule: stage movement and follow-up actions should reflect real business progress. Records should not move stages without milestone evidence, and no active lead should remain without a next action and due date.\n\nCommon misuse to avoid: using memory, side conversations, or private notes as primary tracking instead of CRM updates. If work is not recorded in CRM, other team members cannot reliably continue the customer process.\n\nCustomer-facing impact: strong CRM discipline improves response speed, reduces dropped leads, and creates a more consistent buying experience across team members.","tags":["crm","platform","gohighlevel","sales"]},{"id":"crm-record","title":"CRM as System of Record","topic":"Company and CRM","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-crm-system-of-record","hint":"The CRM is the operational source of truth for lead ownership, stage, and next action; if it is not in CRM, it is not complete.","body":"The CRM is the system of record for lead and deal lifecycle status, ownership, and follow-up accountability. Team members should rely on CRM records as the authoritative timeline for customer engagement.\n\nOperational rule: if it is not recorded in CRM, it is not complete. Notes, calls, outcomes, and stage movement should be entered at time-of-action rather than delayed end-of-day catch-up.\n\nMinimum record hygiene includes accurate contact identity, current stage, assigned owner, and a clear next action with due date. Missing next action is treated as follow-up risk.\n\nWhen conflicting details appear across memory, text threads, or informal notes, resolve against the latest verified CRM timeline and update CRM with clarifying notes so future users inherit the corrected context.\n\nCustomer-facing framing: CRM discipline exists to prevent dropped communication and provide consistent handoff quality, not to add admin overhead.","tags":["crm","system-of-record","data"]},{"id":"rto-terms","title":"Rent To Own Terms","topic":"Payment and rent-to-own","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-financing-rent-to-own","hint":"Rent-to-own is a structured monthly lease path (not interest-based credit financing) that can end in ownership when contract obligations are satisfied, with separate positioning guidance for customer-facing sales conversations.","body":"Technical explanation (ShedCor): Rent-to-own (RTO) is a structured monthly rental/lease agreement, not a traditional credit-interest loan product.\n\nOwnership path: at the end of term (or through approved early payout), ownership transfers to the customer once contract obligations are fully satisfied.\n\nRTO typically does not require a credit check and is positioned to avoid hard-credit-impact concerns seen in traditional financing workflows. Program details still vary and must be confirmed in current contract terms.\n\nReturn/flexibility posture: voluntary return/repo pathways can exist under RTO program rules, generally without the same credit-reporting impact profile as loan default scenarios. Exact treatment must be quoted from current provider terms.\n\nSales-forward consideration (Bennett): RTO is often the best fit for customers who want fast access to a building, lower immediate cash outlay, and no credit-check friction while still wanting an ownership path.\n\nUse-case examples include customers preserving emergency reserves, rebuilding credit, preparing for major purchases (like a home), or choosing flexibility over full upfront cash commitment.\n\nObjection handling guidance: if customers call RTO expensive, acknowledge total-cost reality while reframing value around speed, low down-payment entry, no credit-check friction, and optionality. Present alternatives neutrally (cash, bank loan, traditional financing, RTO) without shame framing.\n\nSales behavior rule: never shame or down-talk RTO users. RTO is a valid product choice; if a customer needs or prefers it, guide them clearly through terms and comparisons so they can make an informed decision.\n\nCustomer-safe phrasing: 'RTO is a lease-style path that gets your building in place quickly with low upfront cash, and it can end in ownership. We can also compare cash and finance options so you choose what fits your goals best.'","tags":["financing","rent-to-own","terms"]},{"id":"rto-vs-finance","title":"RTO vs Traditional Financing (Customer Comparison Guide)","topic":"Payment and rent-to-own","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"rto-vs-traditional-financing-guide-v1","hint":"RTO is a lease-style path with fast access and low upfront friction, while traditional financing is a credit-based loan path; the right fit depends on customer goals, cash position, and credit preferences.","body":"RTO and traditional financing solve similar purchasing goals through different structures. RTO is lease-style with monthly rental payments and potential ownership transfer after obligations are met; traditional financing is a credit-based loan product.\n\nRTO is often preferred by customers who want a quick process, lower upfront cash impact, and no credit-check friction. Traditional financing is often preferred by customers optimizing long-term total-cost profile through credit-based lending.\n\nCash flow lens: customers may choose RTO even when they can pay cash because preserving liquidity/emergency reserves can be more important than minimizing total contract cost.\n\nObjection handling: if a customer says RTO is expensive, acknowledge total-cost concerns and then compare tradeoffs fairly (speed, flexibility, credit profile, upfront cash, and ownership timeline) without steering through shame.\n\nSales behavior rule: present all valid options neutrally (cash, private bank loan, traditional finance, RTO). Match recommendation to customer goals and constraints instead of salesperson preference.\n\nCustomer-safe compare prompt: 'We can review cash, financing, and rent-to-own side by side so you can choose based on monthly budget, credit preference, and total-cost comfort.'\n\nFinal guardrail: exact term details, eligibility, and payout outcomes must come from active contract/program terms for the selected building and provider.","tags":["financing","rent-to-own","comparison","sales_guidance"]},{"id":"electrical","title":"Electrical Packages","topic":"Sizes and styles","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-products-electrical-packages","hint":"Electrical options apply to portable and build-on-site sheds (not steel structures), with customer responsibility for local code verification, permits, and final electrician hookup.","body":"Electrical packages are available on portable sheds and build-on-site sheds. Steel structures do not include electrical package options from steel manufacturers.\n\nNo electrical package is universally standard across all buildings. Greenhouse fan electrical setup is included as part of greenhouse base configuration, not as a separately enumerated package option.\n\nElectrical package structures are plug-and-go style and generally intended for non-occupancy use scenarios. Available package compositions may include combinations of outlets, switches, interior lights, and outdoor/porch-ceiling lights, with some a la carte additions depending on the selected package path.\n\nCode and compliance guardrail: package readiness does not guarantee local code acceptance for every use case. Customer is responsible for verifying local requirements and working with a qualified electrician to bring the system to code for intended usage.\n\nPermit and hookup responsibility: neither Bennett nor ShedCor pulls permits or performs customer-site service hookup work. If an install defect exists from plant setup, service scope is limited to restoring the package to intended plant configuration (including standard 3-prong plug-in configuration).\n\nUsage expectation: where local rules allow temporary plug-in via extension cord, this is usually suitable for basic storage use (for example lighting while accessing stored items) rather than occupancy-grade electrical service.\n\nTimeline note: adding electrical package options does not materially change standard order fulfillment timelines.","tags":["products","options","electrical"]},{"id":"lp-smartside","title":"LP SmartSide Engineered Wood","topic":"Materials and warranty","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-products-materials-lp-smartside","hint":"LP SmartSide is the primary engineered wood siding platform in ShedCor wooden buildings, with related LP materials used for trim, flooring, and roof-decking systems plus upgrade configurations by series.","body":"LP SmartSide is the primary engineered wood siding material used on ShedCor wooden-building wall systems. LP-based trim components are used across series, and LP engineered products are also used in additional building layers (including floor and roof-deck applications).\n\nSeries configuration context: classic and elite wooden lines use upgrade layering paths, including TechShield radiant-barrier options and LP lap-style configurations (same base material family in different profile layouts). Elite-style appearance is achieved through additional lap configuration over core siding paths.\n\nLP engineered wood system performance is positioned for high durability, weather resistance, and improved resistance to common exterior risks such as moisture-related degradation, mold/mildew pressure, and pest exposure when installed and maintained correctly.\n\nCare guidance for long life: maintain exterior finish (paint/urethane touch-up intervals around 10 years as a practical planning point), keep vegetation roughly 1 foot away from siding edges, and preserve airflow/drainage around the building perimeter.\n\nFoundation and set guardrail: buildings should be placed on approved foundation support (company-provided block-and-level or customer-supplied compliant foundation), not directly on bare ground. Elevation and separation from standing water are required to protect floor and structural longevity.\n\nSales language guardrail: position LP as a premium, proven engineered-wood platform and avoid absolute claims that no competitor can match it. Use quality-and-performance framing supported by actual build configuration and maintenance expectations.","tags":["products","materials","lp","smartside","engineered-wood"]},{"id":"portable-overview","title":"Portable Buildings Overview","topic":"Sizes and styles","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-products-portable-buildings","hint":"Portable buildings are enclosed floor-wall-roof structures built on skid/runner systems for transport and set, with standard block-and-level installation included in retail delivery workflows.","body":"A portable building is a fully enclosed structure with floor, walls, and roof built on a skid/runner system for transportability. These are delivered as complete units when access allows transport and set.\n\nStandard installation basis uses concrete block leveling (commonly 2-inch, 4-inch, and 8-inch block combinations with treated shims as needed). On retail/consignment flows this block-and-level set approach is included in delivery MSRP assumptions; wholesale dealer workflows may handle this differently.\n\nSet-quality rule of thumb is support continuity under runner systems, including supported runner ends and practical pier spacing to maintain level and floor integrity. Flat and unlevel sites use different block-stack/shim combinations, but both are set to achieve level placement.\n\nCore use case is accessory/storage building use: lawn and yard equipment, seasonal storage, outdoor recreation gear, and light workshop utility. Finished use concepts like man-cave/she-shed are possible through upgrade paths, furniture, and customer-managed electrical/finish decisions.\n\nPosition portable buildings as a high-quality customizable shell. Final legal use, code compliance, occupancy rules, and utility readiness remain customer responsibilities based on local requirements.\n\nSales/marketing guidance: creative use-case messaging is acceptable when paired with legal-compliance disclaimers so customers understand local permitting and use restrictions can apply.\n\nPortable vs steel distinction: portable buildings include a built floor system on runners; steel structures generally do not include a floor and are installed on customer-provided surfaces (for example prepared ground or concrete), with larger-span advantages but different customization constraints.","tags":["products","portable-buildings","sheds"]},{"id":"roof-styles","title":"Roof Styles Offered","topic":"Sizes and styles","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-products-roof-styles","hint":"Roof styles are Utility (A-frame), Barn (gambrel), and Studio (single slope), available in both portable and build-on-site, with style-specific loft and pricing differences.","body":"Roof style families are Utility (A-frame), Barn (gambrel), and Studio (single slope). These styles are available across both portable and build-on-site workflows; build-on-site is treated as service method extension, not a separate roof-style product line.\n\nEach roof style can price differently at the same footprint. Do not assume style-neutral pricing; confirm current pricebook deltas by model and size.\n\nBarn style includes standard end-loft allowances: 4-foot lofts included on 12-foot-and-shorter units, and 8-foot lofts included on 16-foot-and-longer units. Included loft layouts can be configured as one section or two sections, but must anchor to end-wall structure.\n\nLoft expansion rules: standard loft runs can be extended up to total building length minus 4 feet. U-shaped loft options are available (about 2 feet deep along side walls) to bridge between end-loft and side storage zones.\n\nUtility and Studio styles can also add loft storage, but loft headroom is lower than Barn geometry and no standard included loft package is assumed in these styles.\n\nCustomer-use guidance: Studio style is often preferred for HOA-neighborhood aesthetics due to broad-side frontage and a natural high-side wall that supports door/window layouts well. This is a common preference pattern, not a universal HOA rule.\n\nNever-promise guardrail: confirm loft inclusion, loft length, and style-specific pricing from the active model matrix before final quote language.","tags":["products","roof-styles","utility","lofted-barn","studio"]},{"id":"build-on-site","title":"Build-On-Site Shed Sizes","topic":"Delivery and site","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-products-shed-size-range-build-on-site","hint":"Any shed size can be built on site when delivery is not feasible; build-on-site is commonly required for oversized units or restricted property access.","body":"Build-on-site is available across shed sizes when needed. It is not limited to a small subset of models, and is used to deliver the same product intent when transport/set delivery is impractical.\n\nPrimary triggers for build-on-site are (1) oversized dimensions relative to normal delivery handling (commonly over about 14 feet wide or 40 feet long) and (2) restricted access conditions such as narrow gates, tight spacing between homes, heavy tree cover, or constrained turns.\n\nHOA neighborhoods frequently require build-on-site due to fences and limited path width from street to final placement area. Access review should include the full path, not just final pad dimensions.\n\nOperational expectation: managers (Bennett) and dealers (ShedCor) should gather strong site details and, when needed, perform site verification before final commitment. Crew staging also matters: trucks and trailers should be able to get reasonably close so material carry distance is manageable.\n\nNever-promise guardrail: do not guarantee standard delivery or final install method before access is verified. Present build-on-site as the fallback path that protects schedule and install success when access risk is high.\n\nRelated context for customer Q&A: all steel structures are built on site by steel providers, while shed build-on-site is a method selected when shed delivery constraints make prebuilt set delivery unreliable.","tags":["products","sheds","build-on-site","sizes"]},{"id":"size-range","title":"Portable Shed Size Range","topic":"Sizes and styles","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-products-shed-size-range-portable","hint":"Portable shed quoting usually starts at 6x8 and commonly spans 8x8 through 16x60, with final availability tied to current pricebook models and delivery-access feasibility.","body":"Portable sheds are delivered as prebuilt units when site access allows successful transport and set. A practical quoting baseline is 6x8 at the small end, with most common offerings between about 8x8 and 16x60.\n\nStandard width families are typically 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, and 16 feet. Lengths are usually offered in 4-foot increments, with some model-specific exceptions in the active pricebook.\n\nCurrent pricebook exception handling: some lines include additional small footprints (for example 6x4 and 6x6 utility variants) and some 14-foot/16-foot series include non-4-foot stepping in specific ranges. Always confirm the exact model-size combination in the current pricing matrix before final quote.\n\nPortable pricing also follows next-size-up logic for non-standard lengths. Example: a 10x14 request can be priced the same as a 10x16 if the line is built from standard increment packages.\n\n14-foot-wide units commonly carry additional cost pressure due to transport/permitting complexity. Treat 14-foot-wide delivery planning with extra care during quoting.\n\nDelivery feasibility is as important as model size. Validate gate clearance, turn radius, spacing between structures, tree/line overhead clearance, slope/ground conditions, and path width from road to final placement area.\n\nBuild-on-site guidance by size: consider build-on-site for 14-foot-wide units and for 40-foot length and longer; require build-on-site for 16-foot-wide units and for 40-foot length and longer where delivery constraints make prebuilt set risky.\n\nNever-promise guardrail: do not guarantee portable delivery success until access is verified. Use preliminary ranges first, then confirm final size/method after access review.","tags":["products","sheds","sizes","portable"]},{"id":"siding-tiers","title":"Wood Siding Tiers","topic":"Materials and warranty","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-products-siding-tiers","hint":"Basic, Classic, and Elite tiers are primarily siding/configuration differentiators, with shared core LP material platform and step-up exterior options.","body":"Siding tiers are the primary differentiator between Basic, Classic, and Elite wooden series. Core material platform is shared across tiers, including LP SmartSide wall systems, LP ProStruct floor platform usage, and LP ceiling/deck material paths.\n\nBasic is the base-value tier. Classic is the middle tier and includes TechShield upgrade path components. Elite is the premium tier and also includes TechShield.\n\nElite includes an additional LP lap-siding exterior layer/profile treatment. Current configuration flexibility also allows approved-vendor vinyl substitution at no upcharge in supported workflows.\n\nPricing-position guidance: Basic is least expensive, Classic is mid-tier, and Elite is highest priced. Present the difference as finish/configuration progression rather than implying unrelated structural claims.\n\nSales guardrail: avoid saying non-tier features are uniquely tied to one series when they are shared across tiers. Confirm any vendor-specific substitutions and local availability before final quote.","tags":["products","siding","tiers","basic","classic","elite"]},{"id":"steel-sizes","title":"Steel Structure Size Range","topic":"Steel structures","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-products-steel-size-range","hint":"Steel size options are broad but must be confirmed per manufacturer, use case, and site/engineering requirements before final quoting.","body":"Steel size availability is manufacturer-program dependent and should be quoted from the active provider configuration, not from one universal dimension chart. Longhorn and SteelCraft both support customizable width, length, and height combinations for residential, agricultural, and commercial applications.\n\nBaseline steel sizing standards: 12x20 is typically the smallest standard footprint, with width increments commonly in 2-foot steps and length increments in 5-foot steps. Requests outside those increments are often fulfilled by ordering the next standard size up and cutting down to target dimensions.\n\nPricing guardrail: custom sizes below standard breakpoints can price the same as the nearest standard package because material sets are based on standard size ladders. For example, a smaller-than-12x20 request can still price at 12x20-equivalent structure cost.\n\nFor larger spans, use extra review before final quote. Structures above about 30 feet wide are commonly treated as commercial and may require heavier truss systems, stronger package selections, and stricter engineering/certification confirmation.\n\nBefore giving final dimensions, confirm intended use (carport, garage, barn, or clear-span), installation site readiness, local permit/certification requirements, and any lift/access constraints tied to the selected structure.\n\nLeg-height standard: structures are generally ordered with matching leg height across each side. For sloped sites requiring one side cut down, involve a manager during quoting, order to the taller leg side, and include on-site leg-cut fees as applicable.\n\nSales guidance: provide an initial planning range and then verify quoteable dimensions with the selected manufacturer. Do not promise exact size availability until manufacturer confirmation is complete.\n\nCustomer-safe phrasing: 'We can usually configure this in multiple sizes, and I can narrow it down for you once we confirm your site, use case, and manufacturer options.'","tags":["products","steel","sizes"]},{"id":"steel-overview","title":"Steel Structures Overview","topic":"Steel structures","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-products-steel-structures","hint":"Steel structures are dealer-partner products (not ShedCor-built wood units) with customizable designs, included installation, and manufacturer-specific warranty/process rules.","body":"Steel structures in the Bennett offering are handled through dealer-manufacturer relationships (for example Longhorn Steel Structures and SteelCraft), not through ShedCor wood-building manufacturing. Product families include carports, garages, barns, enclosed metal buildings, and commercial clear-span configurations.\n\nThese structures are typically customizable through manufacturer design workflows and may include 3D design tooling depending on provider. Common use cases include residential storage, agricultural shelter, and commercial applications.\n\nDelivery and installation are generally included by steel providers, while customer responsibilities usually include a suitable prepared site. Concrete, grading, site preparation, electrical completion, and permit compliance are commonly customer-side unless explicitly included in writing for the selected program.\n\nDo not use portable wood-building policy assumptions for steel deals. Payment terms, scheduling, certification, panel coverage, workmanship warranty limits, and site-leveling tolerances vary by steel manufacturer and must be quoted from the active contract/program.\n\nOperational quoting note: steel sizing usually follows standard increment ladders and same-height leg assumptions. Non-standard dimensions or hill-grade installs can require ordering to a larger/taller standard package and applying on-site cut/adjustment fees with manager oversight.","tags":["products","steel","structures"]},{"id":"steel-warranty-routing","title":"Carport and Steel Structure Warranty Routing Guide","topic":"Steel structures","aud":"staff","src":"Company","slug":"carport-and-steel-structure-warranty-routing-guide-v1","hint":"For steel/carport warranty questions, confirm manufacturer and program first; answer from confirmed source terms only, and ask clarifying questions when manufacturer is unknown.","body":"When a customer asks about steel or carport warranty, first confirm both product class and manufacturer program (for example Longhorn or SteelCraft). It is acceptable to answer with manufacturer-specific language once the manufacturer is confirmed.\n\nUse confirmed source context only (active contract terms or the matching policy article). Do not quote warranty duration, coverage type, or exclusions from memory.\n\nIf manufacturer is unknown, ask a clarifying question before giving final warranty terms. Example: 'Are you asking about a Longhorn or SteelCraft structure?' Hold final warranty quoting until manufacturer-specific terms are verified.\n\nGuardrail: never make blanket statements such as 'limited lifetime warranty applies to all steel products.' Warranty can differ by manufacturer, product type, site prep compliance, installation conditions, and damage cause.","tags":["steel","carport","warranty","routing","policy"]},{"id":"steelcraft-terms","title":"SteelCraft Payment, Installation, and Warranty Terms","topic":"Steel structures","aud":"staff","src":"Company","slug":"steelcraft-payment-installation-and-warranty-terms-v1","hint":"SteelCraft terms include non-refundable deposits, milestone payments, strict site-prep requirements, and a 1-year workmanship warranty with defined exclusions.","body":"SteelCraft payment structure is milestone-based. Initial deposit is typically 10% to 15% of retail purchase price (before tax). For orders below $15,000, the remaining balance is due on install date before construction starts. For orders at or above $15,000, a production payment is required before scheduling and the final balance is due at installation.\n\nDeposits are non-refundable, and production payments become non-refundable once production begins. For larger payments, certified funds may be required; card and e-check payments can carry processing fees per contract terms.\n\nSite requirements are strict: site must be prepared, permits completed, and leveling within stated tolerance before installation. Unlevel or obstructed sites can trigger extra charges, service charges, or rescheduling outcomes.\n\nSteelCraft craftsmanship warranty is 1 year (365 days) for structural integrity/workmanship defects. Warranty exclusions include unlevel-site installations, certain visual/cosmetic issues not documented at install, and damage linked to site condition or customer-side noncompliance.\n\nCertification and structural disclaimers apply: non-certified units may not satisfy permitting requirements; certified units follow engineering specs but site-specific engineered drawing scope may be limited. Customer remains responsible for site prep, permitting, and utility marking.","tags":["steelcraft","steel","warranty","installation","payment","policy"]},{"id":"longhorn-terms","title":"Longhorn Payment, Installation, and Warranty Terms","topic":"Steel structures","aud":"staff","src":"Company","slug":"longhorn-payment-installation-and-warranty-terms-v1","hint":"Longhorn terms include scheduling gates, site-leveling thresholds, possible lift requirements, and workmanship/panel warranty limits tied to compliance.","body":"Longhorn installation flow includes early contact after order, pre-install communication, and schedule windows that depend on weather and readiness. Typical timing may be around 2 to 4 weeks after site readiness, but this is not a guaranteed install date.\n\nCustomer is responsible for full site preparation, including leveling, grading, concrete, permits, and utility marking. If site is outside stated level tolerance, additional fees or install-condition changes can apply based on contract terms.\n\nSome larger/taller buildings require customer-provided lift equipment (for example certain 25 to 30 foot wide units at higher leg heights, and larger-span structures). Confirm lift requirement in the selected building spec before scheduling.\n\nLonghorn warranty includes a 1-year workmanship warranty and panel coverage terms that depend on maintenance and roof/design compliance. Common exclusions include modifications, unlevel-site installs, weather-event damage, anchoring issues, and non-approved install conditions.\n\nPayment and ownership clauses are contract-bound: remaining balances are due at install milestones, larger orders may require advance production/scheduling payments, and ownership can remain with Longhorn until paid in full.","tags":["longhorn","steel","warranty","installation","payment","policy"]},{"id":"steel-permits-staff","title":"Steel Structures Permit and Engineering Document Guidance","topic":"Steel structures","aud":"staff","src":"Company","slug":"steel-structures-permit-and-engineering-doc-guidance-v1","hint":"Steel structures usually require permits; customer is responsible for jurisdiction requirements, permit pull, and required document scope, with manufacturer docs provided after signed contract.","body":"Default guidance: steel structures almost always require permits. Some rural jurisdictions may exempt smaller footprints (commonly around 20 feet wide and 30 feet long or less), but this is exception territory and must be confirmed locally.\n\nFor most jurisdictions, larger steel structures require permit package components such as site plans and engineering documentation. Required document depth varies by jurisdiction and complexity.\n\nEngineering document tiers can include generic stamped sets, site-specific sets (address/customer tied), and fully building-specific engineering documents. Costs generally increase from generic to site-specific to building-specific document types.\n\nCustomer responsibility rule: customer must identify required jurisdiction documents and pull permits (or direct us/provider on what is required). Steel providers can often assist or send permit agents, but this is usually expensive due to travel and repeat-jurisdiction visits.\n\nMost jurisdictions allow customer-owner permit pulls without contractor licensing, though exceptions exist. Do not assume owner-pull is universally allowed; verify local rules first.\n\nSales process guidance: proceed with signed contract flow when customer is serious, because steel providers generally do not release engineering documents before contract execution due to liability and misuse risk.\n\nDeposit/refund policy context: published policy posture is generally non-refundable deposits. Manager discretion may be used for hardship or uncontrollable jurisdiction outcomes, while custom-build/late-stage indecision cases are typically held to non-refundable terms.\n\nCustomer-safe response example: 'In most areas, steel buildings require permits. We can help you identify what your county needs and coordinate the right engineering docs after contract setup, but your local jurisdiction decides final permit requirements.'","tags":["steel","permits","engineering_docs","policy","sales_guidance"]},{"id":"steel-permits","title":"Do I Need a Permit for a Steel Structure?","topic":"Steel structures","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"steel-structures-permit-requirements-customer-guidance-v1","hint":"Most steel structures require permits, and local jurisdiction rules determine what plans/documents are needed.","body":"In most areas, yes, steel structures require permits. Some smaller buildings in certain rural jurisdictions may be exempt, but local rules vary and should always be confirmed with your county or city office.\n\nLarger steel structures commonly require permit packages such as site plans and engineering documents. The exact requirement depends on your location and the specific building.\n\nEngineering documentation can often be provided in different levels (for example generic stamped, site-specific, or building-specific), depending on what your jurisdiction requires.\n\nCustomers are responsible for confirming local permit requirements and completing permit applications. If needed, we and the steel provider can help coordinate required documents and next steps.\n\nBest practice: check permit requirements early in the purchase process so your order, engineering documents, and installation timeline stay aligned.","tags":["steel","permits","engineering_docs","customer_guidance"]},{"id":"inventory-lead-times","title":"Expected Inventory Lead Times (Order to Lot Delivery)","topic":"Delivery and site","aud":"staff","src":"Company","slug":"expected-inventory-lead-times-order-to-lot-delivery-v1","hint":"Use rough timeline ranges only, never fixed date promises, and keep proactive communication between sales, manufacturer, and customer.","body":"Standard ShedCor shed order fulfillment timelines are typically 2 to 4 weeks for both customer orders and inventory orders. Timelines can flex during high production periods, but this is the standard planning range.\n\nSteel structure timelines are manufacturer-specific, but often run about 3 to 5 weeks after the installation site is confirmed ready. Ready means concrete is cured, ground is leveled/cleared, and other install blockers are resolved before the steel timeline starts.\n\nShed inventory ships in truckload planning, usually around 40 feet of total building length per load (sometimes more with larger trailers). A single short inventory model is generally not shipped alone; ShedCor admins may request location backlog orders to build full outbound loads.\n\nEscalation starts with the location manager. Ground-level sales/admin team members are expected to raise timing risks early so management can intervene before issues become customer-facing failures.\n\nWeather and site conditions can delay delivery. Rain and soft ground can create major delivery liability, especially when driving heavy trucks onto customer property.\n\nSales communication standard: never promise fixed delivery dates. Give rough timeline ranges, explain known variables, and maintain active communication with both manufacturer and customer to reduce disappointment from predictable delays.\n\nShedCor delivery dates are communicated through the sale-owning salesperson. Steel providers often communicate directly with customers, but sales should still provide oversight so updates do not fall through communication gaps and reflect poorly on Bennett.\n\nSales-forward reassurance language: 'Most orders like yours run about X to Y weeks once required prep is complete. I will stay on top of manufacturer updates and keep you informed at each milestone so there are no surprises.'","tags":["operations","lead_time","delivery","inventory","steel","communication"]},{"id":"two-story","title":"Two-Story Building Availability","topic":"Sizes and styles","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-products-two-story-availability","hint":"Two-story portable sheds are not offered; some steel structures can accommodate second-floor designs.","body":"Two-story portable sheds are not offered; some steel structures can accommodate second-floor designs.","tags":["products","limitations","two-story","steel"]},{"id":"inventory-viewing","title":"Viewing Inventory Without a Quote","topic":"Company and CRM","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-sales-inventory-viewing","hint":"Customers can browse in-stock inventory without submitting personal information via the inventory portal.","body":"Customers can browse in-stock inventory without submitting personal information via the inventory portal.","tags":["sales","inventory","browsing"]},{"id":"idearoom","title":"IdeaRoom Configurator","topic":"Company and CRM","aud":"staff","src":"Company","slug":"kb-sales-tools-idearoom","hint":"IdeaRoom is a 3D design tool used to configure and estimate pricing for wood and steel buildings.","body":"IdeaRoom is a 3D design tool used to configure and estimate pricing for wood and steel buildings.","tags":["sales","tools","idearoom","configurator"]},{"id":"warranty","title":"Limited Lifetime Warranty","topic":"Materials and warranty","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"kb-warranty-limited-lifetime","hint":"ShedCor limited lifetime warranty applies to defined wood shed product lines; greenhouse, doghouse, and steel structure warranties are separate and must not be assumed under this policy.","body":"ShedCor limited lifetime warranty applies to ShedCor wooden shed product lines, including Basic, Classic, Elite, Silver Bullet, and upgraded Colored Bullet series.\n\nGreenhouses are handled under a separate warranty path and should not be answered from the standard limited-lifetime wooden-shed warranty language.\n\nDoghouses currently do not have a defined warranty standard. Do not assume or infer warranty coverage for doghouses unless an explicit doghouse warranty policy is published.\n\nSteel structure warranties are separate from ShedCor wooden-shed warranty policy and must be routed using steel-manufacturer-specific warranty guidance.\n\nBranding note: Bennett warranty messaging for these covered wooden lines reflects/rebrands ShedCor product warranty policy, while underlying fulfillment remains with ShedCor.","tags":["warranty","limited-lifetime","coverage"]},{"id":"anchors","title":"Anchoring and Tie-Down Options","topic":"Delivery and site","aud":"staff","src":"Company","slug":"policy-delivery-anchor-options","hint":"Mobile home anchors and straps are available at an additional cost of $100 per anchor set.","body":"Mobile home anchors and straps are available at an additional cost of $100 per anchor set.","tags":["delivery","anchoring","tie-downs","pricing"]},{"id":"delivery-distance","title":"Delivery Distance Policy","topic":"Delivery and site","aud":"staff","src":"Company","slug":"policy-delivery-free-within-50-miles","hint":"Delivery is free within 50 miles of the purchase location; additional fees may apply beyond that distance.","body":"Delivery is free within 50 miles of the purchase location; additional fees may apply beyond that distance.","tags":["delivery","pricing","distance"]},{"id":"ta-overview-turbo-assist","title":"Turbo Assist — What this app is for","topic":"Using Turbo Assist","aud":"staff","src":"Turbo Assist","slug":"app-overview-turbo-assist","hint":"","body":"## What it is\n\nTurbo Assist is the on-lot helper your team uses every day. It captures new leads while you talk to a customer, sends them to your CRM, gives you grounded answers from your knowledge base, helps you log and learn from social ads, and tracks the small daily habits that grow a sales pipeline.\n\n## Who uses it\n\nSalespeople use it most: new customer intake, follow-ups, daily checklist, ads logging, and quick policy/product look-ups. Trainers and marketing use it for human review, ads library curation, and knowledge base authoring. Owners and admins use the dashboard and admin tools to approve users, manage roles, and watch org health.\n\n## Not a replacement for\n\nTurbo Assist sits **next to** your other systems, not on top of them. Order entry still lives in the POS. The final system of record for leads, opportunities, and pipeline is GoHighLevel (GHL). Calendar invites, RTO contracts, and delivery scheduling live wherever your org normally puts them. Turbo Assist routes information to the right place — it does not replace those systems.\n\n## Who builds it\n\nTurbo Assist is built and operated by **TurboSignCo** — the software, marketing, and advertising arm behind the product. Subscriber organizations (your dealership, for example) use Turbo Assist to run their day-to-day; TurboSignCo handles the platform, the integrations, and the rollout.\n\n## Branding\n\n\"Turbo Assist\" is the platform-default name. Your org can override the display name and brand colors, so you may see your own brand in the header instead. That is normal — the underlying product is the same, and TurboSignCo remains the app's operator regardless of which brand you see on screen.","tags":["app","overview","turbo-assist"]},{"id":"ta-navigation-lead-workspace","title":"Lead workspace — tabs and main areas","topic":"Using Turbo Assist","aud":"staff","src":"Turbo Assist","slug":"app-navigation-lead-workspace","hint":"","body":"## The mode picker\n\nThe top of the screen has a **mode** selector. Each mode is a different workspace: **Leads**, **Sales Console**, **Training Mode**, **Ads Assistant**, **Location Tools**, **Operations / paperwork**, **Knowledge Base**, **Admin**, **Dashboard**, and a few platform-only modes. Which modes you see depends on your role.\n\n## Leads mode\n\nLeads is the default workspace for salespeople. From here you tap **New Customer**, choose **Listen**, and the app fills in name, phone, product, and notes from what you say. Edit anything that looks off, then tap **Send to GHL** when the lead is ready.\n\nYou'll also see your recent leads, a daily checklist of small habits, and location quick links to your lot's pricing and inventory pages.\n\n## The activity dock\n\nA small floating dock sits at the side of the screen. It surfaces things that need your attention — unsent leads, daily checklist items, achievements, and role-specific alerts. If it pulses, something there wants you to look.\n\n## Getting back to the lead list\n\nUse the mode selector to return to Leads from any other mode. Inside Leads, **Close Lead** clears the active customer and shows your recent leads. Pinning a lead keeps it at the top.\n\n## Help inside the app\n\nEvery mode has a small **?** popover with mode-specific tips. Use it any time you forget where a button lives.","tags":["app","navigation","leads"]},{"id":"ta-training-ask-policies","title":"Training — Ask & Policies","topic":"Using Turbo Assist","aud":"staff","src":"Turbo Assist","slug":"app-training-ask-policies","hint":"","body":"## What it does\n\n**Ask & policies** lets you ask questions in plain English — product details, pricing rules, RTO process, warranty, company policy — and the app answers using your organization's knowledge base. Each answer cites its top sources so you can verify or read more.\n\n## How to use it\n\nOpen **Training Mode** and pick the **Ask & policies** tab. Type your question (or use voice dictation if it's enabled). The answer appears with a short list of cited articles. Tap a citation to see the source article.\n\n## How answers are chosen\n\nThe app uses the **active workspace organization** for retrieval. Your org's private articles only appear for your org. Articles shared across the platform (manufacturer- and app-scoped) appear for any eligible org. The system does not check a \"subscription\" — it relies on scope and visibility on each article.\n\n## When answers feel thin\n\n- Add context: say which location, product line, or scenario you mean.\n- If the system surfaces a **contact card**, that means the best answer is a person, not an article. Use it.\n- If the topic is consistently missing or wrong, ask a trainer to propose or fix the underlying article in **Knowledge Base** mode.\n\n## Related\n\n- **Roleplay** uses the same knowledge base for the AI customer's lines.\n- **Contacts** is the full directory view of who handles what.","tags":["app","training","kb","ask"]},{"id":"ta-training-roleplay","title":"Training — Roleplay","topic":"Using Turbo Assist","aud":"staff","src":"Turbo Assist","slug":"app-training-roleplay","hint":"","body":"## What it is\n\nRoleplay is a practice space. You pick a scenario, the AI plays the customer, and you reply as the salesperson. When you're done, the app scores the conversation and shows what to work on.\n\n## How to start a session\n\n1. Open **Training Mode** → **Roleplay** tab.\n2. Pick a **scenario** that matches what you want to practice.\n3. Tap **Start roleplay**. The AI customer opens with the first line.\n4. Reply naturally — type, or use **Spoken reply** to dictate.\n5. Keep going until the conversation feels complete.\n6. Tap **End & score**.\n\n## What the score covers\n\nThe summary grades clarity, knowledge-base accuracy, whether you answered what the customer asked, follow-up questions, open-ended questions, and how you handled concerns. You'll see a short narrative plus the breakdown.\n\nScored sessions count toward your progress strip — that is how you see week-over-week improvement.\n\n## Voice\n\nIf voice is on and your **speak mode** includes answers, the AI customer's lines are read aloud too. Tap **Spoken reply** to talk back instead of typing.\n\n## Resetting\n\nUse **New session** to start over. If you only need a quick policy or product look-up, use **Ask & policies** instead — it is faster than starting a full roleplay.\n\n## Coaching\n\nRoleplay is practice, not a compliance sign-off. Real customer conversations may differ. Trainers may review your scored sessions through the **Reviews** tab when that is enabled for your org.","tags":["app","training","roleplay"]},{"id":"ta-training-reviews-queue","title":"Training — Human review (trainers)","topic":"Using Turbo Assist","aud":"staff","src":"Turbo Assist","slug":"app-training-reviews-queue","hint":"","body":"## Who sees it\n\nThe **Reviews** tab inside Training Mode is visible to **trainers**, **marketing**, **admins**, and **owners** — not to base sales roles. Platform operators (platform_dev / platform_owner) can also access it.\n\n## What you do here\n\nThe Reviews tab lists completed roleplay sessions waiting for human grading. For each one you can:\n\n- Read the full transcript between the AI customer and the trainee.\n- See the AI's auto-score and summary.\n- Add your own grading and coaching notes.\n- Mark the session reviewed.\n\nYour notes become part of the trainee's coaching history. Over time, the patterns you flag also help us improve the AI's prompts and the org's knowledge base.\n\n## Expected cadence\n\nTurbo Assist suggests reviewing roleplay sessions roughly **once a week** so trainees get feedback while sessions are still fresh — but that's a suggestion, not a rule. Your org decides what cadence fits the team's volume and coaching style. Some orgs review every session as it lands; others batch reviews on a fixed day each week.\n\n## Privacy and professionalism\n\nRoleplay transcripts contain practice conversations — sometimes blunt, sometimes awkward. Treat them like any other coaching artifact: confidential to the trainee, their trainer, and org leadership. Do not share transcripts outside the team.\n\n## Knowledge base proposals\n\nIf you spot a recurring KB gap during review, propose or fix the underlying article in **Knowledge Base** mode. Roleplay quality improves directly when org-specific KB content is accurate and complete.","tags":["app","training","review"]},{"id":"ta-ads-assistant-overview","title":"Ads Assistant — Ingestion and Suggestion","topic":"Using Turbo Assist","aud":"staff","src":"Turbo Assist","slug":"app-ads-assistant-overview","hint":"","body":"## What it is\n\nAds Assistant is where your team logs the social ads you run, browses what others have posted, and gets coaching hints on what kind of content tends to perform.\n\n## The three (or six) tabs\n\nWhat you see depends on your role:\n\n- **Sales** see two tabs: **Ingestion** (log a post you ran) and **Suggestion** (browse indexed posts and hints).\n- **Marketing**, **trainers**, **admins**, and **owners** also see **Library** (categories, templates, policies, social mappings), **Feed import** (pull from Meta/Google or paste JSON), and **Org Settings** (platforms, retention, suggestion defaults).\n\n## Logging a post (Ingestion)\n\nPaste the caption text as it appears online, pick the platform (Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, etc.), and set the time the post went live. Save. That is enough to count toward your daily checklist and feed the Suggestion view for everyone in the org.\n\n## Browsing (Suggestion)\n\nThe Suggestion view shows the team's indexed posts plus stats and coaching hints. Use it as a reference before you write tomorrow's post.\n\n## Daily checklist\n\nThe daily checklist counts ads you save **for the calendar day**. That is what makes the \"ads today\" item tick over.\n\n## Workspace org override\n\nIf a platform operator has set a workspace org override, Ads Assistant uses the **scoped** organization — same as Knowledge Base and CRM sync. You'll see a small amber notice when override is on.\n\n## Notes and bookmarks\n\nOrg-scoped notes and bookmarks live under support tools at the bottom of the mode. Use notes for thoughts you want the team to find later.","tags":["app","ads-assistant"]},{"id":"ta-leads-daily-progress","title":"Leads — daily progress and ads activity","topic":"Using Turbo Assist","aud":"staff","src":"Turbo Assist","slug":"app-leads-daily-progress","hint":"","body":"## What counts\n\nYour daily progress strip and the activity dock track a small set of habits that grow a pipeline. The two most visible:\n\n- **Ads saved today** — number of ad posts you logged in Ads Assistant for the current calendar day.\n- **Leads created today** — new customer intakes you started and saved.\n\nOther checklist items may appear depending on your org's setup (call-backs, RTO quotes generated, follow-ups completed, etc.).\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThese numbers are not a scoreboard — they are a habit signal. A salesperson who consistently logs a couple of ads and starts a couple of new leads each day usually has a healthier pipeline a month later than someone who works in bursts. The daily checklist is meant to nudge that rhythm.\n\nTrainers and managers can also see your daily activity, but the goal is coaching, not surveillance.\n\n## Calendar day\n\nProgress is counted by **your local calendar day**, not by 24-hour rolling. The day rolls over at midnight in your time zone. If you log an ad just before midnight, it counts for that day, not the next.\n\n## If a count looks wrong\n\nTry refreshing the page first. If something you saved isn't showing:\n\n- Check that you are in the right **scoped organization** — a workspace override can change which org's data you see.\n- Confirm the timestamp on the saved item is the day you expected.\n- If it still looks wrong, file a bug report from the in-app button.","tags":["app","leads","progress"]},{"id":"ta-knowledge-base-for-users","title":"Knowledge Base — what end users should know","topic":"Using Turbo Assist","aud":"staff","src":"Turbo Assist","slug":"app-knowledge-base-for-users","hint":"","body":"## Two different things share a name\n\n1. **Training → Ask & policies** is where you **read** the knowledge base. Type a question, get a grounded answer with citations. Every salesperson uses this.\n2. **Knowledge Base mode** is where authorized editors **write** the knowledge base. New articles, edits, scope changes, approvals. Not everyone has access — usually trainers, marketing, admins, and owners.\n\nIf you only need to look something up, you want option 1.\n\n## Where articles come from\n\nArticles are grouped by **scope**:\n\n- **For your org only** — pricing, policy, sales scripts, internal process specific to your dealership. Only your org sees them.\n- **Shared product knowledge** — content about products, warranties, and manufacturer-wide info that applies broadly. Shared across organizations.\n- **App help (you're reading one)** — how Turbo Assist itself works. Same for everyone.\n\nThe app handles scoping automatically when it answers a question. You usually don't need to think about it.\n\n## When an answer is wrong or missing\n\nDon't fix it inside the answer — that doesn't update the source. Instead:\n\n- For something specific to your org (prices, policies, scripts), ask a **trainer** or **admin** to update the org article.\n- For shared product/manufacturer info that looks wrong, raise it with your trainer; they can route a proposal up the chain.\n- For app-wide help (like this article), file a bug report from inside the app.\n\nIf you have trainer access yourself, open **Knowledge Base** mode and edit directly.","tags":["app","kb","governance"]},{"id":"ta-workspace-organization-scope","title":"Workspace organization — scoped org and branding","topic":"Using Turbo Assist","aud":"staff","src":"Turbo Assist","slug":"app-workspace-organization-scope","hint":"","body":"## What \"scoped organization\" means\n\nYour account belongs to a **primary organization** (your home dealership or company). Most of the time, that is also the **scoped organization** the app uses for data — leads you create, knowledge base answers, ads, daily checklist, CRM sync.\n\nFor most users, primary org and scoped org are the same. You don't need to think about it.\n\n## When they differ — workspace org override\n\nPlatform operators — TurboSignCo staff who build and operate Turbo Assist — can set a **workspace org override** so they can work on behalf of a specific subscriber organization without changing their account's home org. When override is on:\n\n- Knowledge Base answers use the scoped org's articles.\n- Ads Assistant shows the scoped org's indexed posts.\n- New leads, follow-ups, and CRM sync target the scoped org.\n- Branding (logo, colors, app display name) usually still shows your home org's settings — that is normal.\n\nYou'll see a small amber notice in panels that depend on the scoped org when override is active.\n\n## Should you turn it off?\n\nIf you're a regular sales or trainer user, you should never need to touch override — it isn't exposed to you. If you somehow see it active on your own account and you don't recognize the scoped org, contact an admin before creating new leads — data may land in the wrong place.\n\n## Quick check\n\nThe Ads Assistant help popover and the platform tools show the active scoped org id when override is on. If you're ever unsure, open one of those — they are the source of truth.","tags":["app","workspace","org"]},{"id":"ta-contacts-and-routing","title":"Contacts — who do I call","topic":"Using Turbo Assist","aud":"staff","src":"Turbo Assist","slug":"app-contacts-and-routing","hint":"","body":"## The contacts directory\n\nOpen **Training Mode** → **Contacts** for the full directory. The list combines two kinds of entries:\n\n- **App-user contacts** — other people on your team who use Turbo Assist.\n- **External contacts** — vendors, manufacturer reps, delivery teams, anyone outside the app who handles a piece of the workflow.\n\nSearch by name. Filter by scope (your org, manufacturer-shared, app-wide), source, or location to narrow the list to who should own a specific lane.\n\n## Contact cards next to answers\n\nWhen you ask a people-oriented question in **Ask & policies** (\"who handles delivery scheduling for the south lot?\"), the app may surface a **contact card** beside the article answer. That means the best answer is a person, not a document.\n\n## Why some rows hide details\n\nSome contacts show chain-of-command guidance instead of direct phone or email. That is on purpose — visibility and trust rules in your org may direct you to talk to your manager or trainer first rather than reach out directly. Respect the chain; it usually reflects a real reason.\n\n## When a contact is missing or wrong\n\nContact directories are maintained by people your org has authorized to edit them. By default, that means a user with the **admin** role for your organization. Some orgs may delegate this to a trainer or another designated editor — ask whoever runs your team's app setup if you aren't sure.\n\nIf you don't know who to ask, file a bug report from the in-app button. An admin will pick it up from there.\n\n## Getting back\n\nUse the mode selector to return to Leads or any other mode at any time.","tags":["app","contacts","routing"]},{"id":"ta-notifications-and-checklist","title":"Notifications and daily checklist","topic":"Using Turbo Assist","aud":"staff","src":"Turbo Assist","slug":"app-notifications-and-checklist","hint":"","body":"## Where you'll see them\n\nTurbo Assist nudges live in a few places, on purpose:\n\n- **The activity dock** — a small floating panel that surfaces unsent leads, achievements, role-specific alerts, and your daily checklist. If it pulses, something there wants you to look.\n- **Banners** inside a lead or mode — short, dismissible notices about the current screen (missing lot selection, unsaved changes, override warnings).\n- **The daily checklist** — a small list of habits like \"start a new lead today\" and \"log an ad post.\" Sales users see this; trainer-plus roles see role-appropriate dashboard content instead.\n\n## What to do with them\n\nMost nudges are passive — they show a count or a status. Some are workflow shortcuts: tap an unsent-leads count to send them, tap a checklist item to jump straight to where you'd complete it.\n\nIf a notice is dismissible (an \"x\" in the corner), dismissing it clears it for the current session. The underlying state still updates normally — you're just hiding the visual nudge.\n\n## Sound or pulses you don't want\n\nThe dock can pulse for urgent items. If something is pulsing and you've already handled it, give the dock a second to refresh — the urgency state is recalculated as you act on the underlying item.","tags":["app","notifications","checklist"]},{"id":"ta-report-bug-or-feedback","title":"Report a bug or send feedback","topic":"Using Turbo Assist","aud":"staff","src":"Turbo Assist","slug":"app-report-bug-or-feedback","hint":"","body":"## Where the button is\n\nOn desktop, a small **Report a bug** pill floats at the corner of the screen. You can drag it anywhere comfortable — it remembers where you put it. On mobile, the same entry lives inside the sidebar drawer so it doesn't fight the bottom tab bar.\n\n## What to include\n\nA good bug report has:\n\n- **What you were trying to do** — \"send a lead to GHL,\" \"open Ads Assistant,\" etc.\n- **What happened instead** — error text, weird behavior, a blank panel.\n- **Where you were** — which mode, and if it's a lead, the customer name or lead id helps a lot.\n- **A screenshot** if the issue is visual (the form accepts attachments).\n\nYou don't need to know what caused it. The dev team has tools to trace the rest.\n\n## What happens next\n\nReports go into the **Dev sync queue**. The dev team triages them, fixes what they can, and closes them as work ships. You can revisit your own open tickets through the bug-report panel; it shows status as items move.\n\nFor pilot orgs, expect a response on substantive bugs within a business day or two — sometimes faster, sometimes longer if the issue needs investigation.\n\n## Feedback, not just bugs\n\nThe same form takes feedback and feature ideas. Don't worry about phrasing — \"this would be easier if X\" is a perfectly good submission.\n\n## Urgent production issues\n\nFor true emergencies — leads can't send across the whole org, the app won't load, data looks wrong on multiple accounts — call your **TurboSignCo rep** directly. They have the contact your org was set up with; if you don't have their number, ask your admin.\n\nUse the in-app bug report for everything else, including issues that feel important but can wait until the next business day. Keeping the emergency channel reserved for actual emergencies is what makes it work.","tags":["app","support","bug"]},{"id":"ta-voice-and-dictation","title":"Voice input in Training","topic":"Using Turbo Assist","aud":"staff","src":"Turbo Assist","slug":"app-voice-and-dictation","hint":"","body":"## What's covered\n\nVoice in Turbo Assist works in two ways: **dictation** (you talk, the app types) and **speak-back** (the app reads answers aloud to you).\n\nDictation is the most common. It lives in **Training Mode** under the microphone button on Ask & policies and on Roleplay's **Spoken reply**.\n\n## Enabling the microphone\n\nThe first time you tap the mic, your browser will ask for microphone permission. **Allow** the request — without it, nothing happens.\n\nIf you accidentally denied permission, you'll need to grant it again from your browser's site settings (look for a small mic or lock icon near the address bar) and reload the page.\n\n## Speak-back\n\nIn **Settings → Voice**, you can turn voice on and choose a **speak mode**:\n\n- **Off** — no audio.\n- **Help** — only contextual help is read aloud.\n- **Answers** — KB answers in Ask & policies and the AI customer's lines in Roleplay are read aloud.\n- **All** — both help and answers.\n\nMost salespeople pick **Answers** for hands-busy training on a lot tour. Quiet office? Leave it off.\n\n## Accuracy\n\nDictation works best with clear speech and a stable connection. In a noisy lot, expect a few transcription slips — review the text before you send. The app uses a streaming transcriber, so partial text shows up while you talk and finalizes once you pause.\n\nIf you see the transcription quietly stop in the middle of a long sentence, take a breath and try again — the connection may have hiccuped. The app will auto-reconnect if it can.\n\n## Lead intake voice\n\nThe **Listen** button in Leads mode also uses voice. It's the same microphone permission — once you've allowed it for one feature, the rest work automatically.","tags":["app","voice","training"]},{"id":"ta-display-name-and-branding","title":"App name and branding on screen","topic":"Using Turbo Assist","aud":"staff","src":"Turbo Assist","slug":"app-display-name-and-branding","hint":"","body":"## The default name\n\nThis app's platform-default display name is **Turbo Assist**. You'll see it in the header, help text, and a few places where the app refers to itself.\n\n## Your org may rename it\n\nOrgs can override the display name in their settings. If your dealership has done that, you'll see your custom name in the header instead — for example, \"[Your Dealership] Assist\" or whatever your admin chose.\n\nThe underlying product is the same. Internal documentation may still say \"Turbo Assist\" — that's the canonical name.\n\n## Logo and colors\n\nOrgs can also set:\n\n- A **logo** (shown in the header and on share surfaces).\n- A **primary color** (used on the top bar and major buttons).\n- A **secondary color** (used on badges, links, and accents).\n- A **tagline** (a short line after the brand name in the header — max 20 characters; null = hidden).\n\nIf you see a generic look instead of your dealership's branding, your admin probably hasn't uploaded a logo or set colors yet. That is harmless — it just means the defaults are showing.\n\n## Who edits branding\n\nBranding lives under **Admin** mode → **Org Settings** → **Branding**. Admins and owners can edit it. Trainer and sales roles cannot.\n\nIf your branding looks wrong (old logo, wrong colors), ask an admin to update it. Changes take effect on the next page load for everyone in the org.\n\n## Why the name matters\n\nThe display name is also used in a few automated places — like the source label on lead webhooks to your CRM. If you rebrand, those labels follow.","tags":["app","branding"]},{"id":"ta-browser-and-mobile","title":"Browsers and mobile use","topic":"Using Turbo Assist","aud":"staff","src":"Turbo Assist","slug":"app-browser-and-mobile","hint":"","body":"## Tested browsers\n\nThese four browsers are tested every pilot release. Use a current version of any of them and you'll have the best experience:\n\n- **Google Chrome**\n- **Microsoft Edge**\n- **Safari** (macOS or iOS)\n- **Mozilla Firefox**\n\n## Other browsers\n\nOther modern browsers — Brave, Arc, Vivaldi, Samsung Internet, and similar — usually work because they share an engine with one of the four above. They aren't on the routine test list, so verify the three features Turbo Assist depends on most before relying on one:\n\n- **Microphone capture** — tap **Listen** in Leads or the mic in Training Ask, and confirm dictation works for a sentence or two.\n- **File attachments** — open the bug-report panel and try attaching a screenshot. Confirm it uploads.\n- **General rendering** — open three or four modes and confirm panels look right.\n\nIf those all work, you're in good shape. If anything is off, file a bug report and mention the browser so we know.\n\n## Mobile\n\nThe layout is responsive — sales users routinely run Turbo Assist on a phone or tablet on the lot. A few notes for mobile:\n\n- The activity dock and bug-report button move into the sidebar drawer to keep the bottom tab bar clear.\n- File uploads (bug-report screenshots, ads imports) work but use your phone's image picker — confirm the file before you submit.\n- The microphone uses your phone's mic. The first time you tap **Listen** or **Spoken reply**, you'll need to grant mic permission in your phone's browser.\n\nA progressive web app (PWA) install path is planned, so eventually you'll be able to add Turbo Assist to your home screen and run it like a native app. Until then, just keep a browser tab open.\n\n## Connection\n\nVoice features (Listen, Spoken reply) work best on a stable Wi-Fi or strong cellular connection. If transcription stops mid-sentence, the network is the most likely culprit — pause, give it a moment, and try again. The app auto-reconnects when it can.\n\n## Performance tips\n\n- One tab is fine; ten tabs with voice will compete for resources.\n- If a page feels slow, refresh first.\n- Close inactive leads when you're done so the workspace stays light.","tags":["app","mobile","browser"]},{"id":"ta-integrations-ghl-high-level","title":"CRM sync (GoHighLevel) — what users should know","topic":"Using Turbo Assist","aud":"staff","src":"Turbo Assist","slug":"app-integrations-ghl-high-level","hint":"","body":"## What the integration does\n\nWhen you finish a lead in Turbo Assist and tap **Send to GHL**, the app posts the lead's details — name, phone, email, product interest, location, salesperson, plus any open customer events — to your organization's GoHighLevel (GHL) workspace via an inbound webhook. GHL is the system of record for your pipeline; Turbo Assist is the on-lot capture layer that feeds it.\n\n## What gets sent\n\nA standard lead payload includes:\n\n- Customer contact details (name, phone, email).\n- Product interest and location.\n- Salesperson identity (your account name) and the source label (your org's app display name).\n- Any open follow-up tasks detected during the conversation, so GHL workflows can branch on them.\n- A few diagnostic fields so it's traceable later.\n\nGHL takes it from there — opportunity creation, pipeline stage, automation, calendar invites, all on the GHL side.\n\n## You don't run \"sync\" by hand\n\nThere's no manual sync button you press for normal operation. Each lead's **Send to GHL** is its own self-contained push. The connection between Turbo Assist and GHL is configured once by an admin and runs in the background per lead.\n\n## When data looks stale\n\nIf a lead you sent doesn't show up in GHL, or fields look off:\n\n- Wait a minute — GHL workflows may take a moment to process the inbound.\n- Refresh GHL.\n- Check that your Turbo Assist account is in the right **scoped organization** (a workspace override can route to a different GHL account).\n- If it still looks wrong, file a bug report. Admins can verify the connection on the Turbo Assist side; GHL admin tools can inspect the inbound on theirs.\n\n## Other CRMs\n\nGHL is the only CRM the app talks to directly today. Other systems may receive data via GHL's own integrations downstream.","tags":["app","ghl","crm"]},{"id":"warranty-repair","title":"Warranty repair request","topic":"Materials and warranty","aud":"customer","src":"Company","slug":"warranty-repair-request","hint":"","body":"Customers can submit a warranty repair request via the warranty claim form: ...","tags":["warranty","repairs"]}];
